 <h2 id="stalls">Stalls</h2>
 <p>A stall happens when the wing exceeds its critical angle of attack, not when the airplane is "too slow". The stall occurs at a particular angle of attack; weight, bank angle, load factor, density altitude and airspeed have no direct effect on the stall angle of attack, only on the speed at which the wing reaches it. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('39')}</a> So it is possible to exceed the critical angle of attack at any airspeed, at any attitude and at any power setting. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-9, 5-12')}</a> The recovery follows from the cause: since stall is precipitated by an excessive angle of attack, <strong>the angle of attack must be decreased</strong>. <a href="#source-navweps">{n('39')}</a> Common errors include trying to level the wings or add power before doing so. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-15, 5-21')}</a> Your aircraft's flight manual gives its own stall and departure warnings and recovery.</p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Accelerated stall</strong>: any stall entered at more than 1 G, such as a hard pull in a break turn or a fight. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-19')}</a> In the T-45, pulling to the lift limit puts the jet in heavy buffet. <a href="#source-p1289">{b('4-2')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Cross-control stall</strong>: the critical angle of attack is exceeded with aileron applied one way and rudder the other, often in a skidding base-to-final turn, and it can lead to a spin. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-20')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Spin</strong>: an aggravated stall in which at least one wing is stalled and the airplane autorotates. Its phases are entry, incipient, developed and recovery. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-22–5-24')}</a> Spin and out-of-control recovery follow the aircraft's flight manual.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>For comparison, the FAA treats as an <strong>upset</strong> an event that unintentionally exceeds normal flight parameters for a civil airplane: pitch more than 25° nose up, more than 10° nose down, bank more than 45°, or an inappropriate airspeed within those limits. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-1')}</a> Those numbers are routine attitudes in military flying; what carries over is the idea of recovering angle of attack first.</p>

 <h2 id="envelope">The stall boundary and maneuvering speed</h2>
 <p>Put the turn and the lift equation together: at any speed, CL<sub>max</sub> limits how much load factor the wing can make, n<sub>max</sub> = (V ÷ V<sub>S</sub>)². Plotted against airspeed, that is the stall boundary of a V-n (V-g) diagram. The structure sets a second limit, the limit load factor. Where they meet is the speed fighter pilots call <strong>corner speed</strong>; the civil handbooks call it the <strong>maneuvering speed</strong>, V<sub>A</sub>, the maximum speed at which the positive design load limit can be imposed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('5-19')}</a></p>
 <p>The T-45 training instruction turns this into two kinds of pull. Above corner airspeed, a <strong>load-limit pull</strong>: pull to the G limit, 7.33 G above 5,000 ft. Below it, a <strong>lift-limit pull</strong>: 19–21 units, the rumble of buffet. <a href="#source-p1289">{b('4-3')}</a> Corner speed, 410 KIAS in that jet, gives the best instantaneous turn but also the highest bleed rate, about 8 knots per second. <a href="#source-p1289">{b('4-4')}</a> The <a href={bfm}>BFM module</a> puts these numbers to work.</p>
