 <h2 id="energy">Energy: altitude and airspeed</h2>
 <p>Potential energy is the ability of a body to do work because of its position, here altitude (mgh); kinetic energy is its ability to do work because of its motion, airspeed (½mV²); total energy is their sum. <a href="#source-api">{g('22')}</a> <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-1, 4-18')}</a> With no change in thrust or drag, altitude and airspeed change in opposite directions when energy is exchanged: zoom up and the airplane slows; dive and it speeds up. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-2')}</a></p>
 <p>Dividing by weight gives <strong>energy height</strong>, E<sub>S</sub> = h + V² ÷ 2g, an altitude that measures both. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-7')}</a> The handbook's example: an airplane cruising at 100 kt and 6,000 ft has about the same energy height, 6,500 ft, as one at 240 kt and 4,000 ft, and either could ideally zoom to 6,500 ft. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-8')}</a> Specific excess power, P<sub>S</sub> = (T − D)V ÷ W, is the rate at which energy height can be gained. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-3')}</a></p>
 <p>Thrust adds energy. The Navy's preflight course calls the difference between thrust available and thrust required <strong>excess thrust</strong>: positive excess thrust causes a climb, an acceleration, or both, depending on angle of attack, and so does excess power. <a href="#source-api">{g('60–63')}</a> The controls split the job: <strong>the throttle is the total energy controller; the stick (elevator) is the energy distribution controller</strong>, between altitude and airspeed. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-4')}</a> At low speed and high angle of attack, on the back side of the power curve, there is little or no excess power. <a href="#source-afh">{a('4-5')}</a></p>
 <p>In a fight the same bookkeeping decides who can keep turning. The T-45's best sustained turn rate, 240–330 KIAS, is where the specific excess power curve is zero, and an <strong>energy excursion</strong> is "the conscious sacrifice of airspeed for angles". <a href="#source-p1289">{b('4-4, 5-7')}</a></p>
