 <h2 id="rotor">Rotor wings</h2>
 <p>A helicopter's rotor blades are wings, and everything above applies to each one. The Army's manual states the same laws: lift varies as the square of the velocity, and if angle of attack is increased beyond a critical angle the flow across the top of the airfoil is disrupted and the blade stalls. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-28, 1-29')}</a> What changes is that the wing is spinning, so the airspeed a blade feels depends on where it is around the disk. The Army flies most of the military's helicopters, but the Marine Corps, Navy and Air Force fly them too; the type's operator's manual, NATOPS or -1 gives its own limits.</p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Torque.</strong> Driving the rotor twists the fuselage the other way. Torque must be counteracted, and the antitorque (tail) rotor does this; tandem and coaxial designs turn two rotors in opposite directions instead. Most American-built single-rotor helicopters turn the main rotor counterclockwise seen from above, and too little pedal lets the nose turn in the direction of torque. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-78, 1-79')}</a> In a hover a single-rotor helicopter tends to drift in the direction of tail rotor thrust: <strong>translating tendency</strong>. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-15')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Coning.</strong> As the rotor turns, centrifugal force lifts the blades from their static droop; lift then bends them up into a shallow cone. Excessive coning comes from low rotor rpm or high gross weight, stresses the components and costs lift. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-77')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Hover and ground effect.</strong> To hover the rotor drives a large mass of air downward; this induced flow is the primary cause of the high power needed to hover, and the tip vortices add induced drag. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-88, 1-89')}</a> Close to the surface, ground effect increases rotor efficiency to a height of about one rotor diameter, so a hover in ground effect takes less power than one out of it. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-12')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Translational lift.</strong> Moving forward, the rotor flies into undisturbed air, induced flow falls and the same blade pitch gives more lift. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-24')}</a> Between 16 and 24 knots the disk completely outruns the recirculation of its own vortices: <strong>effective translational lift</strong> (ETL). On the way there, <strong>transverse flow effect</strong> shows as vibration around 12–15 kt and, with a counterclockwise rotor, a slight roll to the right through about 20 kt. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-21–2-24')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <h3>Dissymmetry of lift</h3>
 <p>In forward flight the advancing blade adds the helicopter's airspeed to its own speed; the retreating blade subtracts it. <strong>Dissymmetry of lift</strong> is the resulting difference in lift between the advancing and retreating halves of the disk. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-20')}</a> Left alone it would roll the helicopter toward the retreating side. <strong>Flapping</strong> is the primary means of compensating: the advancing blade flaps up, which reduces its angle of attack, and the retreating blade flaps down, which increases it. Cyclic feathering, the pitch change the cyclic makes around the disk, also creates differential lift. <a href="#source-fm">{fm('1-32, 1-33')}</a> <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-20')}</a> Near the hub on the retreating side the forward speed exceeds the blade's own, and the air meets the blade from its trailing edge: <strong>reverse flow</strong>. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-26–2-27')}</a></p>
