 </section>
 <h3>Hazards</h3>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Retreating blade stall.</strong> At a high forward speed, the retreating blade stalls because of a high angle of attack and slow relative wind. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-20')}</a> High weight, low rotor rpm, high density altitude, turbulence and steep, abrupt turns bring it on; the signs are a low-frequency vibration, the nose pitching up and a roll toward the retreating side. The first step in recovery is usually to reduce collective; aft cyclic only worsens the stall. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-11')}</a> It is why every helicopter has a never-exceed speed that falls with altitude and weight.</li>
  <li><strong>Vortex ring state</strong> (also called settling with power). The rotor settles into its own downwash. It needs a vertical or nearly vertical descent of at least 300 fpm, the rotor using some engine power (20–100 percent), and horizontal speed below ETL; the descent can approach 6,000 fpm. Adding power makes it worse. Recover by increasing airspeed and/or partially lowering collective, or with the Vuichard technique: lateral cyclic combined with more power and antitorque thrust. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-10')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Dynamic rollover.</strong> With a skid or wheel pinned to the ground (a pivot other than the centre of gravity), a rolling moment and thrust greater than weight, the helicopter can roll past the point where cyclic can stop it, about 5–8°. Quickly reducing collective is the most effective way to stop it. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-12')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Low G and mast bumping.</strong> Helicopters rely on positive G for much or all of their control response. In low G a two-bladed teetering rotor can flap until the hub strikes the mast: mast bumping. Recover by first gently applying aft cyclic to restore normal G, then correct the roll. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-14–11-15')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <h3>Autorotation</h3>
 <p>An engine failure does not stop the rotor. Lower the collective at once: that must be done immediately, and it keeps the rotor turning on the upward flow of air as the helicopter descends. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-2')}</a> In the autorotating rotor, the middle of the blade, normally 25 to 70 percent of the radius, is the driving region that turns it; the outer 30 percent is the driven region, held back by drag; and the inner 25 percent is stalled. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('2-26')}</a> Near the ground a flare with aft cyclic cuts forward speed and the rate of descent before touchdown. <a href="#source-hfh">{h('11-4')}</a> The type's flight manual gives its autorotation airspeeds and rotor rpm limits.</p>

