 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>Below corner speed you pull as hard as you can. What limits the turn, and what do you feel?</summary><p>Lift: the wing reaches CL<sub>max</sub> before the G limit. You feel buffet; in the T-45 a lift-limit pull is flown at 19–21 units.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Your jet is 2% heavier than yesterday. How much does the stall speed change?</summary><p>About 1%: stall speed goes with the square root of weight.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why are carrier approaches flown by angle of attack rather than one airspeed?</summary><p>The wing stalls at the same angle of attack at any weight, so an AOA target gives the same margin above the stall whatever the fuel and stores load; the right airspeed changes with weight.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Can a wing stall at high airspeed?</summary><p>Yes. A stall is exceeding the critical angle of attack, which can happen at any airspeed, attitude or power setting: a hard pull at high speed is an accelerated stall.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The airplane is about to stall. What is the first thing to do?</summary><p>Reduce the angle of attack. Levelling the wings or adding power first are common errors.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You halve your airspeed in level flight. What happens to the lift coefficient the wing needs?</summary><p>It quadruples, because lift goes with the square of airspeed. The wing gets it from a higher angle of attack, until CL<sub>max</sub> runs out.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You slow down below L/D<sub>max</sub> speed. Does drag go down?</summary><p>No. Below L/D<sub>max</sub>, induced drag dominates and total drag rises as you slow.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What load factor does a level 60° bank need, and what happens to the stall speed?</summary><p>2 G, and the stall speed is about 41% higher than at 1 G (√2 ≈ 1.41).</p></details>
 <details><summary>At the same bank, you fly twice as fast. What happens to the turn?</summary><p>The radius is four times larger and the rate is half.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Which control changes your total energy, and which trades altitude for speed?</summary><p>The throttle changes total energy; the elevator divides it between altitude and airspeed.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You roll right using aileron alone. Which way does the nose yaw?</summary><p>Left, opposite to the bank: adverse yaw. Coordinated right rudder keeps the ball centred.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a helicopter with a counterclockwise rotor (seen from above), which side of the disk is advancing in forward flight?</summary><p>The right side. Its blades add the forward speed to their own; the left side's blades subtract it.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Flying fast and heavy, you feel a low-frequency vibration, the nose pitches up and the helicopter rolls left. What is happening, and what do you do first?</summary><p>Retreating blade stall. Reduce collective; don't pull aft cyclic, which only worsens it.</p></details>
 <details><summary>You're descending almost vertically at 800 fpm, below ETL, with power applied. Why is adding more power the wrong fix?</summary><p>Those are the conditions for vortex ring state: more power deepens it. Gain airspeed and/or partially lower collective (or use the Vuichard recovery).</p></details>
 <details><summary>The engine quits in cruise. What is the first control movement?</summary><p>Lower the collective immediately to keep rotor rpm and enter autorotation.</p></details>

