 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/flight-fundamentals-faa-evidence.md">Principles of Flight evidence</a>. The turn, stall-speed, drag-curve and V-n equations are standard flight mechanics applied to an invented jet trainer; the tests check them against hand-worked values and the sources' rules of thumb (2% weight for 1% stall speed, 2 G at 60° of bank) and the handbook's energy-height example. The rotor model is the same kind of standard mechanics applied to an invented rotor, checked against hand-worked tip speeds. The FAA handbooks are cited where they explain a point the military texts read for this revision do not cover, or state a law more exactly; for helicopters that is most of the hazards and autorotation, because the extract of FM 3-04.203 stopped at ¶1-89.</p>
 <p>Figures 1–10 are original line drawings, not reproductions of any publication's figure, and are not to scale except where a caption gives the scale.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: fixed- and rotary-wing principles for military pilots of every service. The example jet trainer's and rotor's numbers are invented; T-45 numbers are from its training instruction and apply to that aircraft only. Your aircraft's flight manual governs. This revision is Drafted pending independent source-backed review.</p>
